=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: In which type of business is process costing MOST appropriate?
+ An oil refinery producing a continuous flow of identical output || Separate units cannot be identified.
- A builder constructing individual houses to order || Job or contract costing suits this.
- A printer producing separate customer orders || Job or batch costing suits this.
- An accountancy firm charging clients by the hour || Service costing suits this.
explain: Process costing is used where production is continuous and output is homogeneous, such as chemicals, oil and food.
mistake: Using process costing for customised jobs.
tip: Continuous + identical = process costing.
src: Study text ch8a - process costing (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How is NORMAL loss valued in a process account?
+ At its scrap value, if any; it is not given a share of process costs || Normal loss is expected and absorbed by good output.
- At the full cost per unit of good output || That applies to abnormal loss.
- At selling price || Not used.
- At twice the cost per unit || Not used.
explain: The cost of normal loss is spread over good output; any scrap value reduces process costs.
mistake: Valuing normal loss at full cost.
tip: Normal loss = no cost (scrap value only).
src: Study text ch8a - normal loss (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is an abnormal loss?
+ Actual loss greater than the expected (normal) loss || The excess is unexpected.
- Actual loss less than normal loss || That is an abnormal gain.
- The loss expected in the process || That is normal loss.
- Any loss sold for scrap || Scrap applies to both normal and abnormal loss.
explain: Abnormal loss is valued at the same cost per unit as good output and charged to profit or loss.
mistake: Confusing abnormal loss with abnormal gain.
tip: More lost than expected = abnormal loss.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal loss (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: 1,000 units are input to a process costing $4,500. Normal loss is 10% of input with no scrap value. What is the cost per unit of expected output ($)?
answer: 5
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cost per unit = (process costs - scrap value of normal loss) / expected output
step: Expected output = 1,000 - 10% x 1,000 = 900 units.
step: Cost per unit = 4,500 / 900 = $5.
check: 4500/900
explain: Dividing by expected output spreads the cost of normal loss over good units.
mistake: Dividing by input units ($4.50).
tip: Divide by expected output.
src: Study text ch8a - cost per unit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What are equivalent units?
+ Notional whole units representing incomplete work, used to share costs between finished output and work in progress || They convert partly finished units into full-unit equivalents.
- Units that have been scrapped || Not the meaning.
- Units sold at the same price || Not the meaning.
- Units produced in another process || Not the meaning.
explain: For example, 300 units 50% complete equal 150 equivalent units of work.
mistake: Counting partly complete units as whole units.
tip: Units x % complete.
src: Study text ch8a - equivalent units (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: At the end of a period, 300 units of work in progress are 50% complete for conversion costs. How many equivalent units of conversion are in closing WIP?
answer: 150
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: Equivalent units = units x % complete
step: Units = 300; degree of completion = 50%.
step: 300 x 50% = 150 equivalent units.
check: 300*0.5
explain: Equivalent units are calculated separately for each cost element.
mistake: Counting all 300 units.
tip: Partly done = partly counted.
src: Study text ch8a - equivalent units (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is an abnormal gain?
+ Actual loss LESS than normal loss || The process performed better than expected.
- Actual loss greater than normal loss || That is abnormal loss.
- A profit on selling scrap || Not the definition.
- An increase in selling price || Not related.
explain: Abnormal gains are valued at the full cost per unit and credited, after allowing for scrap value forgone.
mistake: Reversing gain and loss.
tip: Less lost than expected = gain.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal gain (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which is a feature of process costing?
+ Goods finished in stage one are passed on as raw input for stage two || Costs flow through sequential processes.
- Each unit is costed separately to customer specification || That is job costing.
- No losses are expected || Losses are common.
- Costs are collected for each customer || They are collected per process.
explain: Features: continuous production, homogeneous output, costs per process, common losses and WIP.
mistake: Applying job costing features.
tip: Process to process.
src: Study text ch8a - features (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Where is the scrap value of NORMAL loss recorded?
+ As a credit to the process account, reducing process costs || It lowers the cost per unit of good output.
- As a debit to the process account || That would increase costs.
- In the abnormal loss account only || The abnormal loss account deals with abnormal loss scrap.
- As sales revenue of the main product || Scrap is not main product revenue.
explain: The cost per unit formula deducts the scrap value of normal loss from process costs.
mistake: Debiting the process account.
tip: Normal loss scrap = credit process.
src: Study text ch8a - scrap (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Under the FIFO method of valuing process output, which units are assumed to be completed FIRST?
+ The opening work in progress || Opening WIP is finished before new units.
- Units started this period || These follow opening WIP.
- Closing work in progress || Closing WIP is unfinished.
- Abnormal loss units || Not the assumption.
explain: Under FIFO, only this period's costs are used to calculate cost per equivalent unit.
mistake: Averaging opening WIP costs under FIFO.
tip: First in, first finished.
src: Study text ch8a - FIFO (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Input 1,000 units costing $4,500. Normal loss 10% of input, no scrap value. Actual output 860 units.
q: What is the value of the abnormal loss?
+ $200 || Abnormal loss = 900 - 860 = 40 units x $5 = $200.
- $700 || This values all 140 lost units.
- $500 || This values normal loss.
- $180 || This uses $4.50 per unit.
formula: Abnormal loss = expected output - actual output; value = units x cost per unit
step: Expected output = 900; cost per unit = 4,500 / 900 = $5.
step: Abnormal loss = 900 - 860 = 40 units x $5 = $200.
check: (900-860)*5
explain: Abnormal loss is valued at full cost because it is unexpected and should be highlighted.
mistake: Valuing total loss rather than just the abnormal part.
tip: Only the excess loss is costed.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal loss (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q012
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: 2,000 units are input to a process at a cost of $19,200. Normal loss is 5% of input and can be sold as scrap for $2 per unit. What is the cost per unit of expected output ($)?
answer: 10
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cost per unit = (process costs - scrap value of normal loss) / expected output
step: Normal loss = 5% x 2,000 = 100 units; scrap value = 100 x 2 = $200.
step: Expected output = 2,000 - 100 = 1,900 units.
step: Cost per unit = (19,200 - 200) / 1,900 = $10.
check: (19200-100*2)/1900
explain: Scrap value of normal loss reduces the cost borne by good output.
mistake: Forgetting to deduct scrap value.
tip: Costs less scrap of normal loss, over expected output.
src: Study text ch8a - scrap (concept basis; original question)

=== Q013
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Input 2,000 units costing $19,200. Normal loss 5% with scrap value $2 per unit. Cost per unit of expected output $10. Actual output 1,850 units.
q: What is the NET cost of abnormal loss charged to profit or loss?
+ $400 || Abnormal loss 50 units x $10 = 500, less scrap 50 x $2 = 100.
- $500 || This ignores the scrap value of abnormal loss.
- $300 || This deducts too much scrap.
- $1,500 || This values all 150 lost units.
formula: Net abnormal loss = abnormal loss units x (cost per unit - scrap value)
step: Abnormal loss = 1,900 - 1,850 = 50 units at $10 = $500.
step: Scrap of abnormal loss = 50 x 2 = $100; net = $400.
check: 50*(10-2)
explain: The abnormal loss account is debited at full cost and credited with scrap proceeds.
mistake: Ignoring scrap on abnormal loss.
tip: Full cost less scrap.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal loss account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q014
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Input 2,000 units costing $19,200. Normal loss 5% with scrap value $2 per unit. Cost per unit of expected output $10. Actual output 1,930 units.
q: What is the NET abnormal gain credited to profit or loss?
+ $240 || Gain 30 units x $10 = 300, less scrap forgone 30 x $2 = 60.
- $300 || This ignores the scrap value forgone.
- $360 || This adds the scrap value.
- $60 || This is the scrap forgone only.
formula: Net abnormal gain = gain units x (cost per unit - scrap value)
step: Abnormal gain = 1,930 - 1,900 = 30 units at $10 = $300.
step: Scrap revenue forgone (these units were expected to be lost and sold) = 30 x 2 = $60; net gain = $240.
check: 30*(10-2)
explain: Because fewer units were lost, less scrap is available to sell, which reduces the benefit.
mistake: Forgetting the scrap value forgone.
tip: Gain at cost less scrap forgone.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal gain account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q015
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How is the cost of disposing of NORMAL loss treated?
+ Added to the process costs || It is an expected cost of running the process.
- Charged to the abnormal loss account || That is for abnormal loss disposal costs.
- Ignored || It is a cost.
- Deducted from scrap revenue of the main product || Not the treatment.
explain: Disposal costs of abnormal loss go to the abnormal loss account.
mistake: Charging normal loss disposal to profit or loss directly.
tip: Expected costs belong in the process.
src: Study text ch8a - disposal costs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: A process started 5,000 units. Materials cost $20,000 (added at the start) and conversion costs were $13,800. 4,200 units were completed; 800 units remain in WIP, 100% complete for materials and 50% complete for conversion. There were no losses and no opening WIP.
q: What is the cost per equivalent unit of conversion ($)?
answer: 3
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cost per EU = conversion cost / total conversion equivalent units
step: Conversion EU = 4,200 completed + 800 x 50% = 4,200 + 400 = 4,600.
step: Cost per EU = 13,800 / 4,600 = $3.
check: 13800/(4200+800*0.5)
explain: Each cost element has its own equivalent units and cost per unit.
mistake: Using 5,000 units for conversion.
tip: Separate EU for each element.
src: Study text ch8a - equivalent units (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q017
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Materials cost per equivalent unit $4 (WIP 100% complete); conversion cost per equivalent unit $3. 4,200 units completed; 800 units in closing WIP, 50% complete for conversion.
q: What is the value of closing work in progress?
+ $4,400 || Materials 800 x 4 = 3,200; conversion 400 x 3 = 1,200.
- $5,600 || This treats WIP as fully complete for conversion.
- $3,200 || This omits conversion cost.
- $2,800 || This values WIP at 50% for materials too.
formula: WIP value = Σ(equivalent units x cost per EU) for each element
step: Materials: 800 EU x $4 = $3,200.
step: Conversion: 400 EU x $3 = $1,200; total $4,400.
check: 800*4+400*3
explain: Check: finished goods 4,200 x $7 = 29,400; 29,400 + 4,400 = 33,800 = total costs.
mistake: Using one completion percentage for both elements.
tip: Element by element.
src: Study text ch8a - WIP valuation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Under the WEIGHTED AVERAGE method, which costs are used to calculate the cost per equivalent unit?
+ The value of opening WIP plus the costs incurred this period || Costs are averaged across opening WIP and this period.
- Only this period's costs || That is FIFO.
- Only the opening WIP value || Incomplete.
- Only closing WIP costs || Not the method.
explain: Weighted average blends the cost of the previous and current periods.
mistake: Using only current costs under weighted average.
tip: Weighted average = opening + current.
src: Study text ch8a - weighted average (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO statements about process losses are correct?
+ Abnormal loss and abnormal gain are valued at the same cost per unit as good output || Highlighting them at full cost aids control.
+ The scrap value of normal loss reduces the cost per unit of good output || It is credited to the process account.
- Normal loss is valued at the full cost per unit || It is valued at scrap value only.
- Abnormal gains are ignored in the accounts || They are recorded.
explain: Normal loss is expected; abnormal results are highlighted separately.
mistake: Costing normal loss.
tip: Normal = scrap value; abnormal = full cost.
src: Study text ch8a - losses (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each item with its treatment in process costing.
pair: Normal loss => Valued at scrap value only
pair: Abnormal loss => Valued at full cost per unit and charged to profit or loss (net of scrap)
pair: Abnormal gain => Valued at full cost per unit and credited to profit or loss (net of scrap forgone)
pair: Disposal cost of normal loss => Added to process costs
explain: These treatments ensure expected costs are borne by good output and unexpected results are highlighted.
mistake: Valuing normal loss at full cost.
tip: Expected vs unexpected.
src: Study text ch8a - treatments (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: In the four-step process costing framework, what is the FIRST step?
+ Determine output and losses, including equivalent units || The physical flow is established first.
- Complete the process accounts || That is the last step.
- Calculate the total cost of output || That follows the cost per unit.
- Calculate the cost per unit || That follows determining output.
explain: Steps: output and losses, cost per unit, total cost of output and losses, complete the accounts.
mistake: Calculating costs before the physical flow.
tip: Units first, then dollars.
src: Study text ch8a - framework (concept basis; original question)

=== Q022
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: 4,000 units are input to a process. Normal loss is 8% of input. Actual output is 3,600 units. How many units of abnormal loss are there?
answer: 80
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: Abnormal loss = expected output - actual output
step: Normal loss = 8% x 4,000 = 320; expected output = 3,680.
step: Abnormal loss = 3,680 - 3,600 = 80 units.
check: 4000*0.92-3600
explain: Total loss is 400 units, of which 320 are normal and 80 abnormal.
mistake: Calling the whole 400 units abnormal.
tip: Expected minus actual.
src: Study text ch8a - losses (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Why are equivalent units usually calculated SEPARATELY for materials and conversion costs?
+ The degree of completion of WIP often differs between cost elements || Materials may be added at the start; conversion builds up gradually.
- Because materials are never complete || They are often 100% complete.
- Because conversion costs are always 100% complete || They are usually partly complete.
- Because the totals must be different by law || Not a legal issue.
explain: Separate equivalent units give accurate costs per unit for each element.
mistake: Using one percentage for all elements.
tip: Different completion, different EU.
src: Study text ch8a - equivalent units (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: In a process, costs were $36,000 for 6,000 units input. Normal loss is 10% with no scrap value. Actual output was 5,500 units. What is the value of good output?
+ $36,666.67 || Cost per unit = 36,000 / 5,400 = 6.667; but output 5,500 includes a gain of 100 units: 5,500 x 6.667.
- $36,000 || The abnormal gain adds value to output.
- $33,000 || This uses $6 per unit.
- $39,600 || This uses $7.20 per unit.
formula: Output value = actual good units x cost per expected unit
step: Expected output = 6,000 - 600 = 5,400; cost per unit = 36,000 / 5,400 = $6.667.
step: Actual output 5,500 > 5,400, so there is an abnormal gain of 100 units.
step: Output value = 5,500 x 6.667 = $36,666.67 (the gain of $666.67 is credited to the abnormal gain account).
check: 5500*36000/5400
explain: With an abnormal gain, good output is valued at more than total process cost; the excess is the abnormal gain.
mistake: Valuing output at total input cost.
tip: Good units x cost per expected unit.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal gain (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: An exam question gives the degree of completion of opening WIP for each cost element. Which method is usually expected?
+ FIFO || The completion percentages are needed to calculate work done this period on opening WIP.
- Weighted average || It does not need opening WIP completion percentages.
- Job costing || Not process costing.
- Marginal costing || Not the method in question.
explain: If completion percentages for opening WIP are not given, weighted average is usually expected.
mistake: Ignoring the clue in the question.
tip: Completion of opening WIP given = FIFO.
src: Study text ch8a - method choice (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Why is abnormal loss valued at the full cost per unit rather than at scrap value?
+ To highlight its cost to management, since it reflects unexpected inefficiency || It is charged separately to profit or loss.
- Because it is part of normal operations || It is not.
- To increase the value of good output || It does not.
- Because scrap cannot be sold || Scrap may be sold.
explain: Keeping abnormal loss out of good output's cost stops inefficiency being hidden in product costs.
mistake: Spreading abnormal loss over good output.
tip: Abnormal = visible.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal loss (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: A process started 5,000 units with materials of $20,000 added at the start and conversion costs of $13,800. 4,200 units were completed and 800 units are in closing WIP (100% materials, 50% conversion). No losses or opening WIP.
q: What is the value ($) of the 4,200 completed units?
answer: 29400
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Finished output value = completed units x total cost per equivalent unit
step: Materials EU = 5,000; cost per EU = 20,000 / 5,000 = $4.
step: Conversion EU = 4,600; cost per EU = 13,800 / 4,600 = $3. Total = $7 per unit.
step: Finished output = 4,200 x 7 = $29,400.
check: 4200*(20000/5000+13800/4600)
explain: Completed units carry the full cost per equivalent unit of every element.
mistake: Using input units for conversion EU.
tip: Completed units are 100% for all elements.
src: Study text ch8a - output valuation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: In the abnormal GAIN account, what does the DEBIT entry for scrap value represent?
+ Scrap revenue forgone because fewer units were lost than expected || Less scrap is available to sell.
- Extra scrap revenue earned || Fewer units were scrapped.
- The full cost of the gain || That is credited.
- The disposal cost of normal loss || Not this entry.
explain: The net abnormal gain is the full-cost gain less scrap revenue forgone.
mistake: Thinking an abnormal gain increases scrap sales.
tip: Gain = less scrap to sell.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal gain account (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which statement about the output of one process is correct?
+ It is transferred to the next process as an input cost || Costs accumulate as products move through processes.
- It is always sold immediately || It may move to the next process.
- It is never valued || It is valued at cost per unit.
- It is treated as abnormal loss || Not unless lost unexpectedly.
explain: In later processes, transferred-in costs are usually 100% complete.
mistake: Forgetting transferred-in costs in later processes.
tip: Process 1 output = Process 2 input.
src: Study text ch8a - features (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Under FIFO, how is the value of finished output made up?
+ The b/f value of opening WIP plus the cost to complete it, plus the cost of units started and finished this period || Opening WIP is completed first.
- Only units started and finished this period || Opening WIP completed is also output.
- Opening WIP at its b/f value only || The cost to complete must be added.
- An average of all costs || That is weighted average.
explain: FIFO separates the treatment of opening WIP from new units.
mistake: Omitting the cost of completing opening WIP.
tip: Opening WIP: b/f + finish it.
src: Study text ch8a - FIFO (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Put the four steps of the process costing framework in order.
item: Determine output, losses and equivalent units
item: Calculate the cost per unit (per equivalent unit)
item: Calculate the total cost of output, losses and WIP
item: Complete the process and related accounts
explain: Working through the steps in order avoids most process costing errors.
mistake: Skipping the physical flow.
tip: Units, rate, totals, accounts.
src: Study text ch8a - framework (concept basis; original question)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A process has input of 10,000 kg, normal loss of 5% and actual output of 9,500 kg. What does this show?
+ 500 kg normal loss only - no abnormal loss or gain || Expected output is 9,500 kg.
- An abnormal loss of 500 kg || All of the loss is normal.
- An abnormal gain of 500 kg || Actual equals expected.
- An abnormal loss of 9,500 kg || Not correct.
formula: Expected output = input - normal loss
step: Normal loss = 5% x 10,000 = 500 kg; expected output = 9,500 kg.
step: Actual output 9,500 = expected, so no abnormal loss or gain.
check: 10000*0.05
explain: Abnormal results arise only when actual output differs from expected output.
mistake: Treating all loss as abnormal.
tip: Compare with expected output.
src: Study text ch8a - losses (concept basis; original question)

=== Q033
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which exam limitation does the Study Guide state for process costing questions?
+ Losses and work in progress will not be examined in the SAME process || Questions combine them only in separate processes.
- Abnormal gains are not examinable || They are examinable.
- Equivalent units are not examinable || They are examinable.
- FIFO is not examinable || It is examinable.
explain: Knowing this limit helps you recognise what each question is testing.
mistake: Over-complicating exam answers.
tip: Losses OR WIP, not both in one process.
src: Study text ch8a - exam scope (concept basis; original question)

=== Q034
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Process costs are $12,000 for 1,000 kg input. Normal loss is 20% with no scrap value. Actual output is 750 kg. What is the abnormal loss?
+ 50 kg valued at $750 || Expected output 800; abnormal loss 50 kg x $15.
- 250 kg valued at $3,000 || This values all loss.
- 50 kg valued at $600 || This uses $12 per kg.
- 200 kg valued at $3,000 || This is normal loss.
formula: Cost per kg = costs / expected output; abnormal loss = expected - actual
step: Expected output = 800 kg; cost per kg = 12,000 / 800 = $15.
step: Abnormal loss = 800 - 750 = 50 kg x $15 = $750.
check: 1000*0.8-750
explain: Output 750 kg is valued at $11,250; 11,250 + 750 = 12,000.
mistake: Using input to find cost per kg.
tip: Check that output + abnormal loss = total cost.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal loss (concept basis; original question)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: Which formula gives the cost per unit of expected output?
+ (Process costs - scrap value of normal loss) / expected output || Expected output = input - normal loss.
- Process costs / input units || Ignores normal loss.
- (Process costs + scrap value of normal loss) / actual output || Wrong signs and base.
- Process costs / actual output || Distorted by abnormal results.
explain: This rate values good output, abnormal loss and abnormal gain.
mistake: Dividing by actual output.
tip: Expected, not actual.
src: Study text ch8a - cost per unit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: Weighted average method. Opening WIP 500 units: materials $2,000, conversion $900. Costs this period: materials $18,000, conversion $15,550. 4,600 units were completed. Closing WIP 400 units: 100% complete for materials, 25% complete for conversion.
q: What is the cost per equivalent unit of conversion ($)? Give your answer to two decimal places.
answer: 3.50
tol: 0.005
unit: $
formula: Cost per EU = (opening WIP cost + period cost) / (completed units + closing WIP EU)
step: Conversion EU = 4,600 + 400 x 25% = 4,600 + 100 = 4,700.
step: Conversion cost = 900 + 15,550 = $16,450.
step: Cost per EU = 16,450 / 4,700 = $3.50.
check: (900+15550)/(4600+400*0.25)
explain: Weighted average combines opening WIP value with current costs and includes all completed units.
mistake: Leaving out the opening WIP cost.
tip: Weighted average = include opening WIP cost.
src: Study text ch8a - weighted average (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q037
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: Weighted average method. Opening WIP 500 units: materials $2,000, conversion $900. Period costs: materials $18,000, conversion $15,550. 4,600 units completed. Closing WIP 400 units (100% materials, 25% conversion). Cost per EU: materials $4.00, conversion $3.50.
q: What is the value of closing WIP?
+ $1,950 || 400 x 4 + 100 x 3.50 = 1,600 + 350.
- $3,000 || This treats WIP as 100% complete for conversion.
- $1,600 || This omits conversion cost.
- $1,400 || This uses 25% for materials as well.
formula: WIP = Σ(EU x cost per EU)
step: Materials: 400 EU x $4 = $1,600.
step: Conversion: 100 EU x $3.50 = $350; total $1,950.
check: 400*4+100*3.5
explain: Check: completed 4,600 x $7.50 = 34,500; 34,500 + 1,950 = 36,450 = total costs.
mistake: Applying 25% to materials.
tip: Use each element's own completion.
src: Study text ch8a - weighted average (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q038
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: FIFO method. Opening WIP 400 units (100% materials, 60% conversion), value $3,000. Started this period 3,600 units. Costs this period: materials $14,400, conversion $12,530. Completed 3,700 units. Closing WIP 300 units (100% materials, 40% conversion).
q: How many equivalent units of CONVERSION were produced this period?
answer: 3580
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: FIFO EU = work to complete opening WIP + units started and finished + closing WIP EU
step: Complete opening WIP: 400 x 40% = 160.
step: Started and finished: 3,700 - 400 = 3,300.
step: Closing WIP: 300 x 40% = 120. Total = 160 + 3,300 + 120 = 3,580.
check: 400*0.4+3300+300*0.4
explain: FIFO counts only work done in THIS period.
mistake: Counting opening WIP as fully worked this period.
tip: Opening WIP: only the remaining %.
src: Study text ch8a - FIFO (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: FIFO method. Opening WIP 400 units (100% materials, 60% conversion), value $3,000. Started 3,600 units. Period costs: materials $14,400, conversion $12,530. Completed 3,700 units. Closing WIP 300 units (100% materials, 40% conversion). FIFO EU: materials 3,600; conversion 3,580.
q: What is the value of completed output?
+ $28,310 || 3,000 b/f + 160 x 3.50 + 3,300 x 7.50 = 3,000 + 560 + 24,750.
- $27,750 || This values all completed units at $7.50.
- $24,750 || This omits opening WIP.
- $29,930 || This is total cost including closing WIP.
formula: FIFO output = opening WIP b/f + cost to complete + started and finished x full cost
step: Cost per EU: materials 14,400 / 3,600 = $4; conversion 12,530 / 3,580 = $3.50.
step: Complete opening WIP: 160 x 3.50 = $560; started and finished: 3,300 x 7.50 = $24,750.
step: Output = 3,000 + 560 + 24,750 = $28,310.
check: 3000+160*3.5+3300*7.5
explain: Check: closing WIP 300 x 4 + 120 x 3.50 = 1,620; 28,310 + 1,620 = 29,930 = all costs.
mistake: Valuing opening WIP units at the current full cost.
tip: Reconcile to total cost.
src: Study text ch8a - FIFO (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q040
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee calculates cost per unit by dividing process costs by ACTUAL output when there is an abnormal loss. What is the effect?
+ Good output is overvalued, because the cost of abnormal loss is hidden in it || Abnormal loss should be highlighted separately.
- Good output is undervalued || It is overvalued.
- There is no effect || The cost per unit is distorted.
- Abnormal loss is overvalued || It disappears into good output.
explain: Using expected output keeps abnormal results separate for control.
mistake: Dividing by actual output.
tip: Expected output, always.
src: Study text ch8a - cost per unit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO statements about FIFO and weighted average are correct?
+ FIFO cost per unit reflects only the current period's costs || It measures current efficiency.
+ Weighted average blends opening WIP costs with current costs || It averages across periods.
- FIFO and weighted average always give the same output values || They differ when costs change.
- Weighted average requires opening WIP completion percentages || FIFO needs them.
explain: FIFO is better for judging current performance; weighted average is simpler.
mistake: Thinking the methods are identical.
tip: FIFO = this period; WA = blend.
src: Study text ch8a - comparison (concept basis; original question)

=== Q042
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Input is 5,000 kg costing $48,200. Normal loss is 6% of input, sold as scrap at $5 per kg, and normal loss disposal costs are $300. What is the cost per kg of expected output ($)?
answer: 10
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cost per unit = (process costs + normal loss disposal costs - scrap value of normal loss) / expected output
step: Normal loss = 300 kg; scrap value = 300 x 5 = $1,500; expected output = 4,700 kg.
step: Net cost = 48,200 + 300 - 1,500 = $47,000.
step: Cost per kg = 47,000 / 4,700 = $10.
check: (48200+300-1500)/4700
explain: Disposal costs of normal loss increase process costs; scrap value reduces them.
mistake: Ignoring disposal costs.
tip: Add disposal, deduct scrap.
src: Study text ch8a - disposal costs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q043
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A process regularly shows abnormal gains every month. What should management consider?
+ Whether the normal loss allowance is set too high, so expected output is understated || Persistent gains suggest the standard needs revising.
- Nothing, because gains are always good || Persistent gains distort costs.
- Increasing the normal loss percentage further || That would worsen the issue.
- Valuing the gains at zero || They are valued at full cost.
explain: Normal loss rates should reflect realistic expectations.
mistake: Ignoring persistent abnormal results.
tip: Persistent abnormal = revise the norm.
src: Study text ch8a - control (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q044
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Match each feature with FIFO or weighted average.
pair: Uses only this period's costs to find cost per EU => FIFO
pair: Includes opening WIP value in the costs averaged => Weighted average
pair: Requires opening WIP completion percentages => FIFO
pair: Counts all completed units plus closing WIP EU => Weighted average
explain: The two methods differ in how they treat opening WIP.
mistake: Mixing up the methods.
tip: FIFO separates; WA blends.
src: Study text ch8a - comparison (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: In a process with an abnormal gain, a trainee credits the abnormal gain account with the scrap value of the gained units instead of debiting it. What is wrong?
+ Scrap value forgone should be DEBITED to the abnormal gain account, reducing the net gain || Fewer units are available to sell as scrap.
- Nothing - scrap should be credited || The gain means less scrap revenue.
- Scrap should be ignored for gains || It affects the net gain.
- The gain should be valued at scrap value only || It is valued at full cost.
explain: Net gain = gain at full cost - scrap value forgone.
mistake: Thinking gains increase scrap revenue.
tip: Gain = lost scrap sales.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal gain (concept basis; original question)

=== Q046
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: In Process 2, transferred-in costs from Process 1 were $36,000 for 6,000 units, added materials were $11,200 and conversion costs $8,400. 5,000 units were completed and 1,000 units remain in WIP: 100% complete for transferred-in costs, 60% for added materials and 40% for conversion. No losses and no opening WIP.
q: What is the cost per equivalent unit of ADDED MATERIALS ($)?
answer: 2
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cost per EU = cost / (completed units + WIP EU)
step: Added materials EU = 5,000 + 1,000 x 60% = 5,600.
step: Cost per EU = 11,200 / 5,600 = $2.
check: 11200/5600
explain: Each element in a later process has its own equivalent units, including transferred-in costs.
mistake: Using 100% completion for added materials.
tip: Transferred-in is usually 100% complete.
src: Study text ch8a - later processes (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q047
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Why can't individual units be costed separately in process costing?
+ Output is continuous and homogeneous, so costs are averaged over all units produced || Units are indistinguishable.
- Because units are always lost || Losses do not prevent costing.
- Because process costing is illegal for individual units || Not true.
- Because there are no costs to allocate || There are costs.
explain: Process costing averages costs over output for each process.
mistake: Thinking process costing is less precise by choice.
tip: Identical units = average cost.
src: Study text ch8a - process costing (concept basis; original question)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Materials cost $20,000 for 5,000 equivalent units. Conversion cost $13,800 for 4,600 equivalent units. Completed output 4,200 units; closing WIP 800 units (100% materials, 50% conversion).
q: Which statement correctly checks that all costs are accounted for?
+ Completed $29,400 + closing WIP $4,400 = $33,800, which equals total costs || 20,000 + 13,800 = 33,800.
- Completed $33,600 + closing WIP $4,400 = $38,000 || Completed output is overstated.
- Completed $29,400 + closing WIP $5,600 = $35,000 || WIP is overstated.
- Completed $25,200 + closing WIP $8,600 = $33,800 || Both are wrong.
formula: Total costs = finished output + closing WIP (+ abnormal losses)
step: Cost per unit = 4 + 3 = $7; completed = 4,200 x 7 = $29,400.
step: WIP = 800 x 4 + 400 x 3 = $4,400; total = $33,800.
check: 4200*7
explain: Reconciling to total costs is the best way to catch errors.
mistake: Skipping the check.
tip: Always reconcile.
src: Study text ch8a - checking (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A process has normal loss of 5% and abnormal loss this month. The production manager asks for the abnormal loss to be spread over good output "to keep the loss account small". Why should this be refused?
+ It would hide inefficiency in product costs and overstate the value of good output || Abnormal loss must be visible to management.
- Abnormal loss must always be zero || It can arise.
- It would reduce product costs || It would increase them.
- Normal loss would increase || Not relevant.
explain: Separate abnormal loss accounting supports control and accurate inventory valuation.
mistake: Hiding abnormal loss.
tip: Make problems visible.
src: Study text ch8a - abnormal loss (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t3
status: review
important: 1
q: How are equivalent units of closing WIP calculated?
+ Number of WIP units x percentage complete, for each cost element || EU reflect the work done.
- Number of WIP units, regardless of completion || Ignores completion.
- Completed units x percentage complete || Completed units are 100%.
- Input units x normal loss % || Not equivalent units.
explain: Materials are often 100% complete; conversion costs are usually partly complete.
mistake: Using one percentage for all elements.
tip: Units x % complete per element.
src: Study text ch8a - equivalent units (concept basis; original question)
